{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/kernel/kernel.go at exact 8803f89427e4f025f9ad3c027123a6fe20d2ca27 on request/one-verified-world, under promise git:sha1…a6ffa61 on request git:sha1…35d8011\n\nImplements request 555f239cfad9d38d46016fb118161539235d8011 (verify the log once per signing command) at head 8803f89427e4f025f9ad3c027123a6fe20d2ca27, branch request/one-verified-world.\n\nWhat changed. A signing command used to read and verify the whole log three to six times. It now reads it once, and every later check reuses that one verified read.\n\n1. Measurements.\n\nSetup:\n- Frozen frontier: the artroom replay template, refs/seq/589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3 at b3a68ebef36fcb430ecf3f28e934b6cda0d5b147, depth 43,744, signed checkpoint at the head.\n- Probe states made beforehand with the main binary, each a copy of that frontier:\n  - step 1 adds a probe request and builder's promise (head b9cf45780b7b);\n  - step 2 adds a one-path artifact at the probe commit b5a60622 (head 842a99a8fa1d);\n  - step 3 adds an assert by builder for planner to ratify (head e5882d0d41c5).\n- Every run used a fresh APFS clone.\n- Routes: \"local\" is --server -. \"resident\" is a gs serve of the same binary on port 7806 over that clone.\n- Wall time and peak RSS come from /usr/bin/time -l. Each figure is the median of three interleaved runs. The machine was under other agents' load.\n- Raw evidence (per-run stdout, stderr, git process log and summary.txt): scratchpad/lane8/runs2/. Scripts: scratchpad/lane8/bench2.sh and measure2.sh.\n\nCases:\n- ratify, refused: builder ratifies an effective artifact; the fold refuses before signing (exit 1).\n- ratify, accepted: planner ratifies the step 3 assert.\n- state, refused: builder files a promise with no request. On the local route the preflight refuses it (exit 1). On the resident route it is sent with --no-preflight and sequenced as an ineffective record (exit 0).\n- state, accepted: an assert resting on the probe request.\n- artifact (one path) and review-request: both accepted.\n\nBefore and after. Main 69018ecd43a4 (binary stamped vcs.modified=false) against lane head fa478a38d79f:\n\n| case | route | main 69018ecd | lane fa478a38 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| ratify, refused | local | 7.7 s, 623 MB | 7.9 s, 612 MB |\n| ratify, accepted | local | 12.1 s, 757 MB | 8.3 s, 636 MB |\n| state, refused | local | 13.4 s, 628 MB | 7.3 s, 625 MB |\n| state, accepted | local | 25.9 s, 1119 MB | 8.6 s, 632 MB |\n| artifact, one path, accepted | local | 38.0 s, 1125 MB | 9.1 s, 635 MB |\n| review-request, accepted | local | 38.1 s, 1149 MB | 8.6 s, 638 MB |\n| ratify, refused | resident | 7.6 s, 630 MB | 7.8 s, 623 MB |\n| ratify, accepted | resident | 11.3 s, 627 MB | 7.4 s, 615 MB |\n| state, --no-preflight, sequenced ineffective | resident | 28.1 s, 1106 MB | 8.0 s, 629 MB |\n| state, accepted | resident | 34.4 s, 1229 MB | 8.9 s, 845 MB |\n| artifact, one path, accepted | resident | 45.8 s, 1306 MB | 8.5 s, 869 MB |\n| review-request, accepted | resident | 46.1 s, 1299 MB | 8.7 s, 849 MB |\n\nCold whole-log scans per command (git rev-list without a base), main and then lane, for both routes: 0 and 0 for ratify; 1 and 0 for refused state; 2 and 0 for accepted state; 4 and 0 for artifact and for review-request.\n\nConfirmation at the repaired head 8803f89427e4, same method and cases, kept apart from the comparison above. Its runs are labelled h8803r1-3 in runs2/summary.txt.\n\n| case | local | resident |\n|---|---|---|\n| ratify, refused | 7.3 s, 626 MB | 7.2 s, 624 MB |\n| ratify, accepted | 8.2 s, 626 MB | 7.4 s, 629 MB |\n| state, refused (resident: sequenced ineffective) | 7.3 s, 622 MB | 7.7 s, 640 MB |\n| state, accepted | 8.6 s, 636 MB | 8.5 s, 817 MB |\n| artifact, one path, accepted | 8.7 s, 637 MB | 8.5 s, 854 MB |\n| review-request, accepted | 8.6 s, 651 MB | 8.2 s, 866 MB |\n\nNone of these runs made a cold scan.\n\nPeak memory on the resident route is higher than on the local route. Since main 69018ecd, the command streams the resident's 83 MB projection, and it also makes its one local verified read for lookups and admission.\n\n2. One read. With a valid checkpoint, each single-act signing command makes exactly one complete verified read. TestSigningCommandsVerifyTheLogOnce counts the reads.\nA signing command still costs about one complete verified read, about 7-9 s at artroom depth. So the outcome of parent c4c2be71ea1a449f8f4819aa2897a2f26d501fcc, \"much less than a cold fold\", is not met by this lane. One verified load per command meets condition 2, per planner assert 637f7ac660a019cce750bc61080077fee2a678f7.\nTwo further reads are recorded as limits on the architecture page:\n- A chain of two or more acts makes one more read, for its scratch fold. The fold has no copy operation, and the checkpoint material a reader retains holds payloads and attachments but not the signed intent (actor, rests_on, schema) that a record is folded from.\n- In a long-lived process, a seeded submitter makes one full read each time a checkpoint falls due.\n\n3. Replay lookups. Retry lookups and replay checks come from the session reader's verified dedup index, and only while that reader stands at exactly the current head.\n- The key is the kernel's key: target, actor key, namespace and key.\n- A conflicting act under a held key is still refused.\n- Each matched payload is read again by the tree its signature names, and every object is checked against its hash.\n- The index owns its signed bytes: it takes a copy as an event enters it, and a lookup hands out copies, with an intent decoded afresh. Editing anything a load or a lookup returned changes nothing the index judges.\n\n4. Admission. Admission reuses a fold only where both the session fold and the session reader stand at exactly the head it judges. It renders its own copy of that fold, and nothing a snapshot hands out, nested vocabulary lists included, shares storage with it.\nOtherwise admission uses a private reader. The private reader consumes a signed checkpoint but writes nothing: no checkpoint, no ref or pointer repair, and no change to the witness.\nNothing is reused from:\n- a resident's answer;\n- a caller's projection;\n- an audit that failed;\n- a fold that the rollback witness held back.\n\n5. Checkpoint cadence. A process that appends publishes exactly one checkpoint per interval, as main does.\n- Reading back the process's own appends (Reader.CatchUp) verifies the delta but publishes nothing.\n- A seeded submitter collects no checkpoint material. When a checkpoint falls due, it rebuilds by making one full read, and it publishes at that position.\n- TestAnAppendingProcessKeepsTheCheckpointCadence asserts the exact moves for both a resident and a signer. It is red at both earlier repair heads.\n\n6. Tests break each guard. Each guard was broken in a copy: 41 mutants, all red except M8. M8 survives only because an independent second guard blocks the same reuse; with both removed (M8b) it goes red. The new copies are among them: removing each copy turns its own test red. A checkpoint signed by another key is refused on the signing path. Three adversarial reviews ran, at f24c12c7, at fa478a38 and at 4d5d9c2d, and their findings are fixed here.\n\n7. Projections unchanged. After four kinds of accepted act on the frozen copy (artifact, review-request, state, ratify), the projection is byte-identical to a cold fold by main at the same head. This was rechecked at 8803f894 (scratchpad/lane8/ident3/).\n\n8. Suite. go test -p 1 -count=1 -timeout 40m ./... passes at 8803f894 (36 packages). go test -race passes for internal/kernel and internal/app at the same head.\n\nArchitecture. The contracts of the kernel and app layers change as documented in docs/reference/architecture.md, layers 2, 4 and 5. There is no new format, persisted state or package.\nLock order: the sequencer lock, then snapshotMu (only tried, never waited for), then Reader.mu. The admission lock is never held together with snapshotMu.\n\nSecurity. Trust is unchanged. Only this process's own verified reads are reused, and only at a head its reader verified. What the index and the session fold judge is never shared with a caller. Every actor signature is still verified on load, and the checkpoint carries event material only.\n\nSimplification. There is no new framework, and a redundant marker error is removed. The submitter's copied index shares the reader's private signed bytes rather than copying them a second time, because neither index ever writes them.","body":{"commit":"8803f89427e4f025f9ad3c027123a6fe20d2ca27","path":"internal/kernel/kernel.go"}}